WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.197
How this changes the way you read models.

2
00:00:02.477 --> 00:00:06.787
After this lecture, a few things about large models stop being mysterious.

3
00:00:07.067 --> 00:00:22.896
The cost of training relative to inference: each matrix multiply in the forward pass implies two in the backward pass, so a training step costs about three forward passes, plus the memory to store activations (lesson 1’s point about reverse mode).

4
00:00:23.176 --> 00:00:33.651
Why fused kernels matter: the cross-entropy over a vocabulary of tens of thousands is a large tensor operation, and fusing it saves real memory and time.

5
00:00:33.931 --> 00:00:40.693
And why normalization layers and residual paths are designed the way they are: they control how gradients move.

6
00:00:40.973 --> 00:00:44.152
It also sharpens your judgment as an applied engineer.

7
00:00:44.432 --> 00:00:49.787
If a vendor or a paper claims a new layer or training trick, you can ask the questions that matter.

8
00:00:50.067 --> 00:00:51.496
What is its backward pass?

9
00:00:51.776 --> 00:00:53.205
Does it add batch coupling?

10
00:00:53.485 --> 00:00:55.213
Does it preserve gradient scale?

11
00:00:55.493 --> 00:01:01.765
Was it gradient-checked, and does the reported improvement hold with a fair hyperparameter search for the baseline?

12
00:01:02.045 --> 00:01:08.509
Those questions are checks and balances for research claims, in the same spirit as checking model output.
